Audit the document's information structure for human readability.
- When a passage contains procedures and cautions, require either separate concise bullet lists for procedures and cautions, or a concise main-line explanation followed by grouped bullets for supplementary conditions.
- Do not accept a dense paragraph that combines the main procedure, prerequisites, constraints, exceptions, rationale, and verification instructions when those elements can be separated without losing meaning.
- Order supplementary conditions by impact and importance. Use this default priority: scope and applicability, safety and ownership, correctness and contractual constraints, performance and resource limits, then implementation notes. Use a document-specific risk order when it is more important.
- Check that restructuring preserves every requirement, prerequisite, constraint, exception, rationale, and verification condition. Report omissions, duplicated conditions, or changed meaning separately from style issues.
- Reasons may be stated as concise current-design rationale. Flag historical background, work history, chronological change narratives, and meeting-log prose; the dedicated rationale-over-history check also applies.
- Do not flag a short definition, single fact, or genuinely linear sentence merely because it is prose. Flag only passages whose mixed information types or length materially obstruct human reading.

When reporting an issue, cite the section and identify the information types that are improperly mixed or missing. A PASS requires both readable grouping and preservation of the original information.
